From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 05:57:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Run the liquid flow scan on coordinates

BlockFlowing.a is a four-wide, four-deep recursion, so one flowing block walks
up to 340 neighbours per update. Every one of them cost a BlockPosition from
shift(), a second from down(), and an EnumDirection[] plus a Guava iterator
from the for-each - 72 MB/min in profiling, the largest remaining allocation
site in the server. It and getPossibleFlowDirections now take raw coordinates.

Two things made this cheaper than it looked. isBlocked never used the
IBlockData it was handed - it re-reads the block itself - so the argument could
just go. And getPossibleFlowDirections called blockposition1.down() twice on
one line, allocating two positions to ask one question.

updateTick had the same duplication at the top level: down() built six times,
shift() twice, and getLevel(up()) called twice on separate positions. Those
collapse to one each, and the five drain notifications use the coordinate form
of notifyBlockOfStateChange so the position is only built when the neighbour
actually has a physics handler.

The offset arithmetic is the one mechanical risk here - a swapped sign would
compile and then make water flow the wrong way - so NeighbourOffsetTest pins
shift() against getAdjacent*(), the six named neighbours against their deltas,
and opposite() against the negated offset.

